00:31:10.369 --> 00:31:17.430
Um, yeah, um, I will say years ago, a college friend of mine, Beth Schachter, we were at a play.
嗯，对，我得说多年前，我大学的一个朋友，贝丝·沙克特，我们一起去看了一场戏。

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:22.720
It was like an evening of one acts that were, um, it was all absurdist.
那是一个独幕剧之夜，全是荒诞派风格的。

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:28.799
It was kind of like, it was, it was like tin foil, Nanae's cover, you know, people.
有点像是锡纸做的纳奈服装那种感觉，你知道的。

00:31:28.799 --> 00:31:30.480
It was very strange.
那真是非常奇怪。

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:34.400
And my friend, who I went to college with, she directed me a lot in plays in college.
我的这位大学同学，在大学里曾多次执导过我的话剧。

00:31:34.400 --> 00:31:36.359
She leaned over to me in the middle of it.
她在演出中途朝我靠过来。

00:31:36.359 --> 00:31:41.319
And she said, this is the kind of theater our parents fear were making.
她说：“这就是我们的父母最担心我们去创作的那种戏剧。”

00:31:41.319 --> 00:31:44.319
That's a great line.
这句台词太棒了。

00:31:44.319 --> 00:31:46.119
That's really funny.
真的很好笑。

00:31:46.119 --> 00:31:51.119
And this old man, he musted me, fell in love with you.
这个老头，他让我确信，爱上了你。

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:55.890
New York City.
纽约市。

00:31:55.890 --> 00:32:08.569
And now commercials, end of commercials, back to show.
现在是广告时间，广告结束，回到节目。

00:32:08.809 --> 00:32:15.529
It's so hard because when you're in something, it's almost like you can badmouthe your own family.
这太难了，因为当你身处其中时，这就好比你可以说自己家人的不是。

00:32:15.529 --> 00:32:18.769
But when somebody else badmouts your family, you're like, wait a second.
但当别人说你家人的不是时，你就会想：等一下。

00:32:18.769 --> 00:32:24.369
Like, if I have work on something and somebody goes, I remember even writing for Dave Letterman, which was a great honor.
就像，如果我参与制作了某样东西，然后有人说——我还记得当年为大卫·莱特曼写稿，那是一项极大的荣誉。

00:32:24.369 --> 00:32:25.210
I think Dave's a legend.
我觉得大卫是个传奇。

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:26.049
I love Dave.
我爱大卫。

00:32:26.049 --> 00:32:29.960
Every now and again, I tell someone that and they'd go, oh, I hate him.
每隔一段时间，我跟别人说起这个，他们就会说，噢，我讨厌他。

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:32.000
And I go, well, I don't know what to say to that.
然后我就说，好吧，对此我不知道该说什么。

00:32:32.000 --> 00:33:01.140
I write stuff for that show and I love that and I would take it personally that they were dissing Dave, most people love Dave by the way, but it's just weird, it's like I'm not Dave, I didn't create that show, I was just a writer, I was 22 year old writer but I would take it personally and it's like you wanna, it's so hard because you have to believe in the thing you're doing so it's always that dance of like, can I slightly indicate I didn't like the show but I thought the person was amazing in the show and I think it's always, you just can't say it.
我为那个节目写过东西，我很喜欢，而且别人要是贬低戴夫，我会觉得是在针对我——顺便说一句，大多数人都很喜欢戴夫——但这就很奇怪，就好像我不是戴夫，我没有创作那个节目，我只是个编剧，一个22岁的编剧，但我却会把这当成针对我个人的攻击。这真的很纠你懂的，太难了，因为你必须坚信你正在做的事情，所以这里面总有一种拿捏：我能不能稍微暗示一下其实我不太喜欢这个节目，但我认为这个人在这部剧里的表现太棒了？我觉得这总是……你就是没法直说。

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:09.259
And I'm of the feeling that like, especially if someone is a professional or has their eyes set on that.
而且我的感觉是，特别是如果对方是个专业人士，或者一心想要朝这个方向发展的话。

00:33:09.259 --> 00:33:14.740
Like, you know, I never would tell someone like, maybe you should consider doing something else.
就像，你知道的，我绝不会对别人说：也许你该考虑干点别的。

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:22.180
Like, that's something, if that comes about, that's gonna be, you know, there's a famous Ed Norton story that he went in and auditioned.
这种事吧，如果真的发生了，那将是……你知道的，有个著名的关于爱德华·诺顿的故事，他以前去试镜。

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:27.180
I'll say who it was, it's George Ann Walken, who was this casting director for years.
我直说是谁吧，是乔治安·沃肯，她做了多年的选角导演。

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:28.579
I don't know if she still does.
我不知道她现在还在不在做。

00:33:28.579 --> 00:33:34.102
But I think he was auditioning for the public something he'd been at it for a while.
但我想他当时是在为公共剧院的什么项目试镜，他已经坚持了一段时间了。

00:33:34.102 --> 00:33:40.807
And he's told the story many times, but he auditioned and she said, look, I think you're really smart.
他讲过很多次这个故事，他试镜完后，她说，听着，我觉得你真的很聪明。

00:33:40.807 --> 00:33:41.925
I like you a lot.
我很喜欢你。

00:33:41.925 --> 00:33:45.738
I think you need to start looking at something else to do.
我觉得你需要开始考虑做点别的了。

00:33:45.738 --> 00:33:56.059
And like a week later, he booked Primal Fear and he became a movie star and got an Academy where so there's always a thing of like, we don't know what's bad.
结果大约一周后，他接下了《一级恐惧》，成了一名电影明星，还获得过奥斯卡提名，所以世事难料，我们根本不知道什么叫烂。

00:33:56.059 --> 00:33:57.059
Nobody knows anything is the rule.
规则就是：谁也不知道什么会火。

00:33:57.059 --> 00:34:02.500
And also like, you're an actor, you're an artist, like some things are gonna hit, some things aren't.
而且还有，你是个演员，你是个艺术家，总有些作品会反响热烈，有些则不会。

00:34:02.500 --> 00:34:10.860
I think that it's better to keep the enthusiasm up of like, I love you, man, I'm here to support you.
我觉得最好还是保持热情，告诉对方：我爱你，兄弟，我支持你。

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:14.699
I want you to, you know, to do well.
我希望你能，你知道的，发展得很好。

00:34:14.699 --> 00:34:15.780
There's a great story.
有个很棒的故事。

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:27.980
I think it was John Krasinski told about that he kind of dissed a movie, either to Paul Thomas Anderson or near Paul Thomas Anderson and Paul Thomas Anderson pulled him aside and he was like, never criticized movies.
我想是约翰·卡拉辛斯基讲的，说他曾经贬低过一部电影，要么是对着保罗·托马斯·安德森说的，要么就是在保罗·托马斯·安德森附近说的，然后保罗·托马斯·安德森把他拉到一边对他说：永远不要批评电影。

00:34:27.980 --> 00:34:31.340
Like, especially, you know, in public.
尤其是，你知道的，在公开场合。

00:34:31.340 --> 00:34:35.539
He said, even bad movies, people are trying to make something good and interesting.
他说，哪怕是烂片，人们也是在努力创作一些美好且有趣的东西。

00:34:35.539 --> 00:34:36.380
People were so hard.
大家真的很不容易。

00:34:36.380 --> 00:34:37.719
People were so hard.
大家真的很不容易。

00:34:37.719 --> 00:34:39.139
You got to follow the person's lead.
你得顺着对方的反应来。

00:34:39.139 --> 00:34:41.260
If someone comes off stage and goes, didn't that show suck?
如果有人下台后说：刚才那演出是不是太烂了？

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:47.980
You can go like, well, you were great, but yeah, maybe, if the person comes off and doesn't offer that up, just you got to be like, go with God.
你可以说：嗯，你演得很棒，不过确实，也许……但如果那个人下台后根本没提这茬，那你只能顺其自然、听天由命了。

00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:54.019
And there's probably a couple of people in your life that you should keep on tap, who are like, you'll give it to your straight.
生活中可能确实有那么几个人，你应该随时保持联系，他们会跟你说实话。

00:34:54.019 --> 00:34:56.099
like you're my honesty people.
就像，他们是我的“直言不讳”智囊团。

00:34:56.099 --> 00:35:13.380
But for the most part, I think like, you know, I mean, this was, this was stacked up for maximum, you know, this, we're getting into a more serious discussion about something that this episode actually doesn't exactly interrogate because Lily's play clearly sucked.
但在大多数情况下，我觉得，我的意思是，这些都是为了达到最大效果而故意堆砌的……你知道的，我们现在正在深入探讨一些本集并没有真正去剖析的东西，因为莉莉的剧确实烂透了。

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:15.900
Barney's play intentionally sucked.
巴尼的剧则是故意拍得很烂。

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:17.900
But I liked that Barney got a little sucked in.
但我喜欢巴尼有点被带进去的那种状态。

00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:22.219
He actually cared about his performance and he's like, I, it's too many guys didn't get to see the end of it.
他其实很在乎自己的表演，他说，我……有太多人没看到结局了。

00:35:22.219 --> 00:35:31.579
He like clearly wants to be able to finish his show, which I think I liked that moment, because that's the, even he wants to finish his stupid show, because goddamn it, he's come this far.
他显然很想把自己的演出演完，我觉得我挺喜欢那个时刻的，因为那就是……连他都想演完他那部愚蠢的剧，因为该死的，他都走到这一步了。

00:35:31.579 --> 00:35:32.900
I liked that we showed that side.
我很喜欢我们展现出了他这一面。

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:35.980
And also, there's something about Barney that became clear.
而且，巴尼身上还有一点变得很清晰。

00:35:35.980 --> 00:35:41.579
It's like, this is an aspect of his gambling addiction, which is that he has to win.
这就好比，这是他赌瘾的一个体现，那就是他必须赢。

00:35:41.579 --> 00:35:42.940
He has to win.
他必须赢。

00:35:42.940 --> 00:35:47.860
It's like they don't put money on it, but the bet is I can make you tell me this sucks, Lily.
这就好像他们没有拿钱下注，但赌注是：我能让你承认这很烂，莉莉。

00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:50.139
Yeah, yeah, and he will not stop.
对，对，而且他会没完没了。

00:35:50.139 --> 00:35:52.980
Yeah, it is, it is his gambling addiction.
对，这就是，这就是他的赌瘾。

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:58.980
He must win, that's really true, and he has to, but then he, I love that there's a little part of him that kind of loves his own show.
他必须赢，这真的很真实，他非赢不可，但随后，我喜欢他内心其实还有一小部分是挺喜欢自己的演出的。

00:35:58.980 --> 00:35:59.820
Yeah.
对。

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:02.780
And then he gets slapped in the face by Marshall, so he gets his come up and it's like that.
然后他就被马歇尔当众扇了一巴掌，所以他得到了应有的报应，就是那种。

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:08.380
And there is no clearer review than slapping the face of a person performing.
没有什么比扇正在表演的人一巴掌更能说明问题的了。

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:09.219
Yeah.
对。

00:36:09.219 --> 00:36:12.699
Yeah, I love that Marshall ended that end of the show for Barney.
对，我喜欢马歇尔为巴尼的演出画上了这样一个句号。

00:36:12.699 --> 00:36:16.219
I love that Ted gets pulled up for the curtain call, I love that he has to show.
我喜欢泰德被拉出来谢幕，我喜欢他不得不面对这一切。

00:36:16.219 --> 00:36:23.880
There was something that is fun to play about like, I am, I don't want this to be happening, but I have to act like it's all okay.
那种感觉很有趣，就是去演绎：我根本不想经历这一切，但我还得装作一切都很好的样子。

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:30.800
Like, whenever you get to opposing things that kind of pull you apart, you know, that's always really fun to play.
你看，每当你面临两种相互拉扯、让你左右为难的对立情绪时，你知道的，演起来总是非常有意思。

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:35.920
When you have to wear a mask, you know, yes, yeah, totally, totally.
当你不得不戴着面具的时候，你知道的，是的，没错，完全是这样。

00:36:35.920 --> 00:36:37.800
And that's what the whole episode's about, which I love.
而整集讲的就是这个，我非常喜欢。

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:40.400
And I think it's a good, it's a good Ted and Robin episode.
而且我觉得这是一集很好的泰德和罗宾的故事。

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:50.612
It kind of, it both moves their future forward a little bit and raises the stakes and they're going to live together now, which is a huge part of the episodes to come, but also shows you like a little bit of trouble, right?
它既让两人的未来稍微推进了一点，拉高了赌注——他们现在要住在一起了，这是接下来剧集的重要部分——同时也向你展现了一点点隐患，对吧？

00:36:50.612 --> 00:36:53.039
There's a little bit of trouble rooming there, right?
同居生活里开始滋生一些小摩擦了，对吧？

00:36:53.039 --> 00:36:55.742
It's definitely without spoilering anything.
绝对是这样，就不剧透了。

00:36:55.742 --> 00:36:59.119
It foreshadows things to come at the end of the season.
它为这一季末尾将要发生的事情做好了铺垫。

00:36:59.119 --> 00:37:01.466
I got to highlight a great needle drop.
我必须得重点夸一下这里绝妙的配乐插入。

00:37:01.466 --> 00:37:03.330
I did not get into the stooges.
我以前并不迷“丑小子”乐队（The Stooges）。

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:06.519
I was not into the stooges at this moment in my life.
我人生中的这个阶段并不迷“丑小子”。

00:37:06.519 --> 00:37:08.323
Carter and I love the stooges.
卡特和我很喜欢“丑小子”。

00:37:08.323 --> 00:37:14.880
One of the best shows I've ever seen is Rebecca Carter and I went to see Iggy Pop at Irving Plaza in the 90s.
我看过最棒的演出之一，就是90年代我和丽贝卡、卡特一起去欧文广场看伊基·波普（Iggy Pop）的演出。

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:19.572
And he was at that point, you know, what it was in his 50s or whatever he was.
当时他已经，你知道的，五十多岁还是多少岁了。

00:37:19.572 --> 00:37:28.415
And he gave the most fucking energetic punk rock show, you know, 25 years after, you know, when he would have been considered in his prime of that.
但他呈现了一场他妈的能量爆棚的朋克摇滚演出，你知道的，那可是在他被认为处于全盛期25年之后。

00:37:28.415 --> 00:37:29.197
He is so raw.
他实在是太生猛了。

00:37:29.197 --> 00:37:33.528
And that is like one of my favorite, um, Stooges songs, Reiki Pop songs.
那绝对是我最喜欢的……“丑小子”乐队的歌、伊基·波普的歌之一。

00:37:33.528 --> 00:37:34.851
I want to be your dog.
《我想做你的狗》（I Wanna Be Your Dog）。

00:37:34.851 --> 00:37:39.363
And the idea I got was so proud of it watching it last night with her back.
昨天晚上和贝卡一起看的时候，我油然而生一种骄傲感。

00:37:39.363 --> 00:37:42.551
I said, no other CBS sitcom ever used a Stooges song.
我说，CBS没有任何其他的情景喜剧用过“丑小子”的歌。

00:37:42.551 --> 00:37:45.800
That's It's essentially a song about like kind of S&M.
这本质上其实是一首关于某种S&M（虐恋）的歌。

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:46.639
Yeah.
对。

00:37:46.639 --> 00:37:47.480
It basically is one that song.
基本上就是那一首。

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:48.320
So it's like a five o'clock.
所以就像是五点钟。

00:37:48.320 --> 00:37:53.920
Also one cool thing about that song is like, you know how the hi-hat in drumming, like the hi-hat kind of keeps the tempo?
另外关于那首歌很酷的一点是，你知道打鼓时的踩镲（hi-hat）吗，踩镲有点像是在维持节奏？

00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:57.880
In that song, if you listen to that song, the hi-hat part is a single piano note.
在这首歌里，如果你仔细听，踩镲的部分其实是一个单音钢琴键。

00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:00.159
There's just one piano note going ding, ding, ding, ding, ding.
只有一个钢琴键在“叮、叮、叮、叮、叮”地敲着。

00:38:00.159 --> 00:38:01.000
Oh, wow.
哦，哇。

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:06.039
It's kind of like basically the hi-hat part, but it's as if the drummer is keeping time on one piano key, the ding, ding, ding.
它基本上起到了踩镲的作用，但听起来就像是鼓手在用一个琴键来打拍子，那个“叮、叮、叮”。

00:38:06.039 --> 00:38:07.280
I love that about that song.
我很喜欢这首歌的这一点。

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:09.079
It's such a weird production choice.
这是一个非常诡异的制作选择。

00:38:09.079 --> 00:38:11.760
I don't know if David Bowie, God, I should know that.
我不知道是不是大卫·鲍伊……天哪，我应该知道这个的。

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:14.480
I don't know if David Bowie produced it out or not, but it's just a really cool.
我不知道是不是大卫·鲍伊制作的，但这真的很酷。

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:19.239
Listen for that one piano note, and I want to be your dog, but- David Bowie produced Iggy Pops.
大家听听《我想做你的狗》里那个单音钢琴键，不过——大卫·鲍伊制作过伊基·波普的专辑。

00:38:19.239 --> 00:38:20.079
He didn't do stooges.
他没做过“丑小子”的。

00:38:20.079 --> 00:38:21.079
He did Iggy Pops, I think solo.
我想他做的是伊基的个人专辑。

00:38:21.079 --> 00:38:22.239
He did Iggy solo, I think.
我想他做的是伊基的个人作品。

00:38:22.239 --> 00:38:23.400
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:38:23.400 --> 00:38:35.000
But there's also something funny, like when you literalize it, when it's like, I want to be your dog, and then you see Robin walking five men with collars and leashes.
但也有搞笑的地方，比如当你把它字面化的时候，当歌词是“我想做你的狗”，然后你看到罗宾遛着五个戴着项圈和链子的男人。

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:37.519
It takes the entendre away.
这就把双关意境给消解了。

00:38:37.519 --> 00:38:39.199
It makes it a single entendre song.
它变成了一首单关歌曲。

00:38:39.199 --> 00:38:47.127
It's no longer about what it's- Something else is barely a double entendre in the song, but we still made it even less.
它不再是关于那种……歌里原本勉强算是个双关的东西，但我们把它变得更加直白了。

00:38:47.127 --> 00:38:47.627
Yeah.
对。

00:38:47.627 --> 00:38:48.127
Yeah.
对。

00:38:48.127 --> 00:38:51.591
It was a great great music choice Great needle drop.
这是个非常非常棒的音乐选择，绝佳的配乐切入。

00:38:51.591 --> 00:39:05.647
I have really forgot that we did that and that should be on the list of like fucking cool music we put on like the stooges were on at 830 on CBS after King of Queens and before two and a half men and like a CSI.
我真的忘了我们用过这个，这绝对应该列入“我们用过的那些他妈超酷的音乐”清单里——“丑小子”乐队的歌竟然在晚上八点半的CBS播放，在《皇后区国王》之后、《好汉两个半》和某部《犯罪现场调查》之前。

00:39:05.647 --> 00:39:14.573
